\chapter{Values and truth}
MuLL has seven value kinds: nil, Boolean, number, string, list, record, and
function. \api{G:Type(value)} returns their names as strings. Values are
dynamically checked at the operation that needs a particular kind.

\begin{mullcode}
G:Assert(G:Type(nil) == "nil");
G:Assert(G:Type(42) == "number");
G:Assert(G:Type([1,2]) == "list");
G:Assert(G:Type({answer: 42}) == "record");
G:Assert(G:Type(fn(){ return nil; }) == "function");
G:Assert((0 or 99) == 0);
G:Assert(("" or "fallback") == "");
\end{mullcode}

\section{Only two values are false}
Only \api{nil} and \api{false} are false in conditions. Zero, an empty string,
an empty list, and an empty record are true. This makes \api{or} useful for
fallback without discarding a valid numeric zero or an intentionally empty
collection. \api{and} and \api{or} return operands, not coerced Booleans.

Numbers are finite IEEE-754 binary64 values. Integers are exact only within
the representable range; the language does not provide a separate arbitrary
precision integer kind. Arithmetic that produces infinity or NaN is an error.
Division and remainder by zero are errors rather than special numeric values.

Lists and records compare structurally. Values of different kinds are unequal;
there is no implicit conversion between a string and a number. Functions
compare by callable identity. Use \api{G:Str} when an explicit display string
is needed, and \api{G:Len} for a string's byte count or a collection's size.
